Another approach is to use a dynamic programming algorithm, which involves breaking down the problem into smaller subproblems and solving each subproblem only once. This can be done using a table that stores the weights of all possible subgraphs of $G$ up to a certain size, and then using this table to calculate the weights of larger subgraphs. This approach can be more efficient than the brute-force approach, but it may still be impractical for very large graphs.

